    "reasoning_content": "Excellent. Dyno is highly relevant — note \"s_alpl\" in the card = ALPL receptor! Dyno-9zh engages \"human Alkaline Phosphatase (ALPL) receptor for BBB-crossing\". So the card's \"phenotype labels [s_alpl]\" refers to Dyno's ALPL-targeting capsids. So Dyno Therapeutics is clearly a programme this card's result would feed — they do \"several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno\" and \"integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms\".\n\nSo programme 1: Dyno Therapeutics. Their pipeline step targeted: mechanism-of-action / receptor-engagement assessment of candidate capsids (which capsids cross BBB via which receptor; structure-based design for new BBB-crossing mechanisms). Quote from Dyno-yp2 launch (2026-01-12): \"Dyno's novel generative AI models, trained on billions of in vivo measurements, most recently accelerated with NVIDIA hardware and software... integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms with exceptional efficiency.\" And \"Dyno is advancing capsids that engage additional BBB transport pathways, including several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno, as well as approaches involving alkaline phosphatase (ALPL)\".\n\nActivity: active — Dyno-yp2 launched Jan 12, 2026; Dyno-9zh launched 2026.\n\nProgramme 2: Gradinaru Lab (Caltech) / APPRAISE-AAV — the direct origin of the method the card scales up. Step: ranking capsid peptides vs receptors by AF-Multimer pose scoring before in vivo work. Activity: APPRAISE GitHub active (Boltz-1/Chai-1/OpenFold3 support added — recent commits); Mol Ther 2024 paper. Need most recent activity: check GitHub commits page for latest commit date. Also recent Gradinaru lab papers 2025/2026. Let me check the commits page.\n\nProgramme 3 (optional): Capsida Biotherapeutics — BBB-crossing engineered capsids for CNS (Lilly partnership). Or Voyager TRACER. Or maybe better: the Huang et al. 2023 PLoS Biol \"receptor-directed screening\" work is Gradinaru lab too. Alternatively \"Bhatt lab\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, a third strong candidate: **Delaware? no**. Let me think about \"consortia\": There might be a \"BRAIN Initiative\" consortium for AAV tools. Or \"Addgene\"? Not a consumer of this result.\n\nAnother candidate: **Denali**? no capsid. **Voyager Therapeutics** — TRACER capsid discovery for CNS; they identify BBB-crossing capsids and have \"capsid-receptor\" work? Voyager's pipeline: TRACER uses in vivo screening of AAV capsid libraries in NHP. They also have \"AAV vector engineering\" and published on \"cross-species BBB penetration\". Their step is in vivo screening, not structural complex enumeration. The card's result (which capsid-receptor binding signatures mark transcytosis) would change how they interpret/deprioritize hits — but their pipeline step is different.\n\n**Capsida Biotherapeutics**: \"engineered capsids... cross the BBB\"; their platform: \"high-throughput screening + AI\"? Their site: \"Capsida's platform... structure-guided capsid engineering\"? Let me check.\n\nActually, a third option more aligned: the **Huang/Gradinaru \"receptor-directed screening\"** is the same lab. Another lab: **Bhatt?** no. The **Bhatt lab** is wrong.\n\nWhat about **Alcyone Therapeutics**? They do AAV capsid engineering for CNS (biodistribution). Less relevant.\n\n**4D Molecular Therapeutics (4DMT)**: Therapeutic Vector Evolution (TVE) — in vivo selection of AAV capsids with tissue-specific promoters; they have \"AAV.CNS\" — e.g., 4D-125? Actually 4DMT's CNS program: \"4D-125 for FSHD?\" no that's muscle. They had \"4D-150\"? Hmm. 4DMT's \"AAV.CNS1\" capsid for CNS via IV? Not sure about receptor mapping.\n\nAlternatively, an academic programme explicitly doing \"molecular mapping of AAV capsid–receptor interactions on BMECs\": the **Bhatt lab**? no. What about the lab of **Bhakti?** No. There's the lab of **Brian Bhatt?** no.\n\nThere is: **\"BBB receptor atlas\"**? Hmm.\n\nPerhaps the best third: **Capsida Biotherapeutics** (industry; Lilly/AbbVie partnerships; \"engineered capsids that cross the BBB\"); or **Voyager Therapeutics** (TRACER; Neurocrine partnership; published \"engineered AAV capsids with enhanced BBB crossing\").\n\nLet me look at what the card's step is: \"exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer)\" to determine \"which AAV variants transcytose across the BBB versus remain intracellular, and for which capsid-receptor binding signatures\". Programmes that would use: those deciding which capsid-receptor pairs to advance — i.e., labs/companies with BBB-crossing AAV pipelines where mechanism/receptor matters.\n\nAlso the SHAY 2023 paper explicitly says CA4 is conserved in primates and enables design of human brain-penetrant vectors — so the Gradinaru lab has an active program on CA4-targeted vectors. Also the PLoS Biology 2023 (Huang et al.) \"nearly all published AAV capsids that cross BBB in mice use LY6A or LY6C1 which are not present in primates.\"\n\nLet me now:\n1. Get APPRAISE GitHub commit activity (latest commit date) — for activity field.\n2. Get Gradinaru lab recent news/papers (2025-2026).\n3. Get Capsida or Voyager page quotes.\n4. Check \"already done\": Has anyone run exhaustive pose sampling on capsid-receptor panels? Check Ding et al. 2024 (APPRAISE) — they may have done multiple seeds/poses and studied convergence. Check \"7WQO\" — Jang et al. structural basis (modeling of PHP.eB-LY6A complex). Also there might be a recent2025/2026 paper doing exhaustive AF-Multimer on AAV capsid-receptor pairs. Let me search.\n\nAlso check for \"transcytosis\" assays on BMEC: Shay et al. used in vivo; maybe someone did BMEC transcytosis + receptor binding mapping. The root question mentions \"brain microvascular endothelial cells\" — relevant programmes might use BMEC transcytosis assays (e.g., \"Nagel?\"; \"Schnyder?\"; \"Pardridge\"?). Hmm.\n\nLet me search for already-done evidence and for Capsida/Voyager."
