by Dyno\". Have they run capsid-receptor complex enumeration? They say \"integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms\". Not clear they ran exhaustive docking/AF-Multimer. Status: unknown/partly — they've validated receptors and phenotype but no public record of exhaustive pose enumeration. I'll say \"no\" with claim about no public evidence... but need to be careful: \"Check whether that programme, or work it relies on, has already run this experiment.\" For Dyno, \"unknown\" may be fair since much is proprietary. But the 2026-01-12 press release describes their practice: \"integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms with exceptional efficiency\" — structure-based design is used but no public statement of exhaustive capsid–receptor pose enumeration or a shared 3-fold motif. I'll say \"no\" with claim \"no public record...\" Hmm, \"unknown\" is more accurate for proprietary work. I'll use \"unknown\" for Dyno and Capsida? For Capsida, they have \"In Vitro Binding Platform... Plate-Based Receptor Binding Detection Bead-Based Receptor Binding Detection\" and \"predicted binding site\" mentions (\"Sequence identity is calculated across full protein and is not restricted to predicted binding site\") — implying they do structural prediction of binding sites. They've done receptor binding assays at scale but not published exhaustive AF-Multimer pose enumeration. Status \"partly\"? Their ASGCT abstract \"Identification of Multiple Novel Blood-Brain Barrier Receptors ... via an Integrated AAV Capsid Engineering Platform\" — that maps capsid↔receptor pairings. The card's experiment is about pose geometry depth and a shared motif. So \"partly\" for Capsida (they mapped capsid-receptor pairings and use predicted binding sites) and \"partly\" for Gradinaru (10-model APPRAISE + one validated pose), \"unknown\" for Dyno (proprietary, but head-to-head ALPL capsid comparisons done).\n\nLet me now verify a few specific quotes/URLs:\n\nFor Gradinaru/APPRAISE step quote — the Shay 2023 Sci Adv methods sentence: \"Each model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from each competition.\" That's a great quote for how they do the step today. URL: https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adg6618 or PMC10115422. Also the APPRAISE README quote: \"APPRAISE... is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\"\n\nAlso for the validated PHP.eB–LY6A pose (already_done for Gradinaru): Ding et al. 2024 Mol Ther. Let me get a quote about validating PHP.eB–LY6A pose with contacts. The card cites \"peptide Pro5', Phe6', LY6A Ala58\" and \"capsid-fragment RMSD to PDB 7WQO at most 1.5 Å (published engineered-loop RMSD is 1.36 Å)\". 7WQO is likely from Jang et al. 2022 (Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB, Mol Ther Methods Clin Dev) — a model of PHP.eB loop fit into cryo-EM density? Let me check the Ding 2024 paper for details of the PHP.eB-LY6A validation and model counts. Let me fetch the Molecular Therapy paper (Ding 2024) or its PMC version.\n\nAlso, is there a recent (2025-2026) paper from anyone running exhaustive AF-Multimer on AAV capsid-receptor panels? Let me search briefly for \"AAV capsid receptor AlphaFold-Multimer 2025 transcytosis\" and \"BAV? capsid docking GPI3-fold\". Also check for \"Bhatt? lab\" no.\n\nAlso need \"practice\" claims for Dyno about how they handle the step today: scale (billions of in vivo measurements per month; LEAP), tools (generative AI models on NVIDIA DGX/Hopper GPUs, TransformerEngine), people (Roche/Novartis/Sarepta/Astellas partners). Quotes: from the Dyno-yp2 launch: \"Dyno's novel generative AI models, trained on billions of in vivo measurements, most recently accelerated with NVIDIA hardware and software, using Hopper GPUs on DGX Cloud and libraries such as TransformerEngine, integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms with exceptional efficiency.\" And \"In parallel with its work on TfR1-mediated delivery, Dyno is advancing capsids that engage additional BBB transport pathways, including several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno, as well as approaches involving alkaline phosphatase (ALPL)\".\n\nFor the card's step in Dyno's pipeline: \"identify/validate which receptor each capsid engages and whether that engagement yields BBB crossing\" — i.e., mechanism-of-moA determination for their multi-mechanism portfolio. Quote from Dyno-9zh: \"Engages human Alkaline Phosphatase (ALPL) receptor for BBB-crossing, supporting translation to humans.\" Or the gene-delivery page: \"Best capsid when compared head-to-head with other ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP.\"\n\nNow for \"already_done\" for Dyno: the fact they say \"several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno\" and head-to-head ALPL capsid comparisons in mouse and NHP suggests they've done receptor-mechanism validation experiments but not public exhaustive docking. Also note s_alpl in the card = ALPL phenotype labels — suggests the card's test set includes ALPL-targeting capsids from Dyno's context. Interesting.\n\nFor Capsida quotes:\n- Step: \"We have identified novel human blood-brain barrier receptors that bind our engineered capsids and are conserved between primates and humans.\" (capsida.com/science-innovation/)\n- Or from ASGCT 2025 deck: \"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\" and \"In Vitro Binding Platform Demonstrates Reproducible Capsid/Receptor Binding Data Consistent with Expression Profiles\" and \"NR004: endothelial-specific\". And \"Sequence identity is calculated across full protein and is not restricted to predicted binding site\" — indicates predicted binding sites.\n- Activity: June 11, 2025 IND clearance for CAP-003; plus Aug 2025 corp deck; and publications page May 2025 ASGCT. Also \"1st AbbVie opt-in ($40M) achieved\" and \"Two clinical programs in 2025 / Third clinical program in 2026\". Most recent: Capsida's2025 announcements. Let me check if there's anything more recent (2026) from Capsida. Search quickly.\n\nAlso should I double-check Gradinaru lab recent activity beyond APPRAISE (e.g., 2025-2026 papers)? The APPRAISE repo commits Apr 2026 suffice as \"most recent release/commit\". Good.\n\nLet me also verify the Ding 2024 paper details on model counts and validation of PHP.eB–LY6A pose (for \"already_done\" claims and gaps). Fetch the Molecular Therapy article or PMC.\n\nAlso, I should check whether anyone has already done \"exhaustive capsid-receptor complex enumeration... shared 3-fold-axis GPI motif marks transcytosis\". Search for recent literature.\n\nLet me also confirm the \"s_cat\" reference (\"insert location already fixes the 3-fold site\") — VR-VIII insert at 3-fold axis (from Jang/Xu papers). Fine.\n\nNow for gaps: things like — no public transcytosis-rate phenotype labels across a capsid panel in BMECs (cards' labels [s_alpl] may come from limited data); GPI-anchored receptor avidity weak contacts evade prediction; 12 capsids/6 receptors limited; poses are peptide-level not full capsid; species (mouse LY6A/LY6C1 not in primates); no programme publishes exhaustive pose-depth data; ALPL is a membrane-anchored enzyme (not GPI?) — ALPL is GPI-anchored in its native form (tissue-nonspecific ALPL is GPI-anchored). Interesting: ALPL is GPI-anchored! So Dyno's ALPL-targeting capsids fit the GPI-receptor hypothesis. Good to note.\n\nLet me fetch the Ding 2024 Molecular Therapy article for the model-count quote and the PHP.eB–LY6A validation. The Cell link: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(24)00219-3. Cell may block. Try PMC. Search for \"APPRAISE Ding 2024 Molecular Therapy PMC\".\n\nAlso search for recent AAV BBB receptor mapping2025/2026 (e.g., \"AAV capsid BBB receptor transcytosis 2026\") to check already-done and find any additional programme (e.g., Voyager). And check Capsida news 2026 for activity recency."
