nisms (ALPL, TfR1, discovered receptors) — the card's \"which capsid-receptor binding signatures mark transcytosis\" would feed their mechanism-diversification strategy. Capsida identifies novel human BBB receptors and does capsid/receptor binding assays — also strong.\n\nI think the best three: Voyager (ALPL/VCAP-102; transcytosis phenotype), Gradinaru/APPRAISE (the computational step being scaled), Dyno (multi-mechanism BBB portfolio with structure-based engineering). That covers academic method, industry with ALPL mechanism (directly matches s_alpl), and industry with multi-receptor portfolio. Capsida could be mentioned in gaps, or I could swap Dyno for Capsida. Hmm. Actually \"for which capsid-receptor binding signatures\" — Dyno says they \"integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms\" — matches the card's lever about binding-signature motifs. Capsida's \"In Vitro Binding Platform... Capsid/Receptor Binding Data\" + \"predicted binding site\" also matches.\n\nThe instruction says \"Pick one to three.\" I'll pick three: Voyager, Gradinaru Lab (APPRAISE), Dyno. And note Capsida and Lir in gaps? Gaps are for factual gaps (\"\\\"gaps\\\": [\\\"\\\"]\") — likely gaps in the evidence/answer, not extra programmes. I can put things like \"no public data on pose-depth scaling\" etc.\n\nHmm, but which is the best consumer of \"exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration result\"? Let me think about each programme's \"pipeline step the card targets\":\n\n1. **Voyager (TRACER → VCAP-102)**: Pipeline step = determining mechanism of action (which receptor a TRACER-evolved capsid uses, and whether binding initiates transcytosis). The card targets the in silico capsid-receptor complex modeling that could replace/complement receptor identification + transcytosis assays. Their own words: \"We identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model.\" Activity: active — Moyer et al. May 2025; also Voyager ongoing (need recent activity2025/2026 — e.g., pipeline updates, Neurocrine partnership). Let me search for Voyager recent news 2026.\n\n2. **Gradinaru Lab**: step = AF-Multimer pairwise peptide-receptor competition ranking (APPRAISE) used to classify receptor-dependent capsids. Quote: \"Each model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from each competition.\" or from Ding 2024: \"we conducted each competition using two different inputs with reversed orders (POI 1-POI 2 and POI 2-POI 1). Consequently, when AlphaFold-Multimer was used, 10 independent models were generated for each competition\". Activity: APPRAISE commits Apr 9, 2026 (1.3.0, Boltz-1/Chai-1/OpenFold3); Lab paper Cell Reports Oct 2025 (AAV-hCA4-IV77).\n\n3. **Dyno**: step = mechanism selection for BBB-crossing (which receptor each capsid engages; multi-mechanism portfolio). Quote from Jan 2026 release. Activity: Dyno-yp2 Jan 2026; Dyno-9zh 2026.\n\nFor \"already_done\":\n- Voyager: partly — they identified ALPL and showed transcytosis in a cell barrier model for VCAP-102, but no exhaustive pose enumeration across a panel. Status \"partly\".\n- Gradinaru: partly — 10 models per competition; one validated PHP.eB–LY6A pose; false negatives (9P08/LY6A, 9P36/CA-IV); Nat Commun 2024 used 5 AF-Multimer models of LRP6 complexes; no depth-scaling study, no shared-motif test across panel. Status \"partly\".\n- Dyno: unknown — proprietary; public materials show head-to-head ALPL capsid comparisons and receptor-mechanism portfolio but no public exhaustive docking study. Status \"unknown\" or \"no\". I'll say \"unknown\" with claim that no public record exists of exhaustive pose-depth enumeration; their public work validates mechanisms in vivo.\n\nNow, key quotes to verify/collect:\n\n**Voyager**: Need a URL for the Moyer 2025 paper (Molecular Therapy: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymthe.2025.04.046) and a recent activity announcement. Also Voyager's own site description of TRACER (\"RNA-guided TRACER AAV capsid evolution platform\"). Let me fetch voyager therapeutics site + recent news. Also the paper page (Mol Ther) for quotes about the cell barrier model and receptor ID. And maybe a quote about \"in vitro BBB transcytosis assay\" scale.\n\nAlso — was this work with Neurocrine? VCAP-102 is Voyager's wholly-owned? Voyager has \"VY-TAU\"? Actually Voyager's pipeline: VCAP-102 (AAV, for...?) Hmm. VCAP-102 is Voyager's \"next-generation AAV capsid\". Let me search for Voyager activity 2026 and VCAP-102.\n\n**Dyno**: URLs: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-launches-dyno-yp2-a-top-performing-tfr1-mediated-aav-capsid-to-further-diversify-cns-delivery-portfolio (Jan 12, 2026). Quote: \"Dyno is advancing capsids that engage additional BBB transport pathways, including several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno, as well as approaches involving alkaline phosphatase (ALPL), a compelling and distinct mechanism with translational relevance across species.\" Activity quote: same page or the gene-delivery page \"Launched in 2026, the Dyno-9zh is a top-performing capsid... Engages human Alkaline Phosphatase (ALPL) receptor for BBB-crossing, supporting translation to humans.\"\n\n**Gradinaru**: URLs: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE (README quote + commits Apr 2026); https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adg6618 or PMC10115422 (10 models quote; false negatives quote); https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(24)00219-3 (Ding 2024; HT-APPRAISE scale: \"The HT-APPRAISE screening took 24 h using three parallel Google Colaboratory GPU sessions\"; \"approximately 0.1–1 GPU minute per peptide-LY6A model\").\n\nFor \"already done\" for Gradinaru: Shay 2023 quote \"Some false negatives were also observed, however, as in 9P08 with LY6A or 9P36 with CA-IV.\" and Ding 2024 \"10 independent models were generated for each competition\". Nat Commun 2024: \"Five structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles\" — deeper sampling on LRP6 complexes with cryo-ET validation but not a panel-wide motif test.\n\nAlso check: has any programme tested \"transcytosis vs intracellular retention\" per capsid-receptor pair in BMEC monolayers at scale? Voyager did a \"cell barrier model\" for VCAP-102-ALPL (Moyer 2025). The Lin 2025 Cell Reports notes \"The process of receptor-mediated BBB crossing by AAVs involves three key steps: receptor binding, cellular uptake, and transcytosis... While the mechanistic details of cellular uptake and transcytosis are poorly understood\". Good gap material.\n\nNow let me get:\n1. Voyager TRACER page + VCAP-102 / recent activity.\n2. Moyer 2025 abstract page quotes (already have abstract; get URL confirmation + maybe more quote on cell barrier model).\n3. Dyno gene-delivery page for practice quotes (already have from search).\n4. Maybe Capsida as backup.\n\nAlso for gaps: species differences (LY6A/LY6C1 mouse-only; CA4 primate differences), peptide-level APPRAISE not full-capsid docking, weak avidity contacts, no standardized transcytosis phenotype labels, BMEC models vary (iPSC-BMEC vs primary), ALPL is GPI-anchored (fits motif hypothesis) etc.\n\nLet me search for Voyager."
