    "reasoning_content": "Excellent. I now have rich material. Let me decide the three programmes:\n\n1. **Voyager Therapeutics (TRACER capsid discovery + ALPL NeuroShuttle)** — the card's s_alpl phenotype labels come from Moyer 2025 (Voyager). Step: identify the vascular receptor a TRACER capsid uses and show it can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis (cell barrier model) — i.e., decide which capsid–receptor pairs transcytose. Activity: active — Q2 2026 results (Aug 6, 2026), VY1706 IND on track Q2 2026, AAIC 2026 data, Dec 31 2025 10-K. Most recent: \"Voyager Reports Second Quarter 2026 Financial and Operating Results\" (2026-08-06) — VY1706 expected to begin dosing Q4 2026. \n\nWait — today's date is Oct 21, 2026. So Q2 2026 results (Aug 2026) is recent. Good.\n\n2. **Gradinaru Lab (Caltech) — APPRAISE-AAV / receptor-directed AAV engineering** — the method being scaled. Step: AlphaFold-Multimer pairwise peptide–receptor competition ranking (10 models/competition) to classify capsid receptor usage before in vivo. Activity: active — APPRAISE repo commits Apr 9, 2026 (\"1.3.0 package\", Boltz-1/Chai-1/OpenFold3 support); Cell Reports Oct 2025 AAV-hCA4-IV77 paper.\n\n3. **Dyno Therapeutics** — multi-mechanism BBB capsid portfolio (ALPL, TfR1, conserved candidate receptors); \"integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms\". Activity: active — Dyno-yp2 launched Jan 12, 2026; Dyno-9zh and Dyno-n96 launched 2026 (ASGCT 2026); © Dyno 2026.\n\nThat's a solid three. Capsida can be noted in gaps or omitted. Gaps can mention Capsida/Lir? No — gaps should be evidence gaps. I'll mention in gaps things like: no public pose-depth scaling data; BMEC transcytosis phenotype labels sparse; peptide-level vs full-capsid; species gaps; and note that other programmes (Capsida, Lir Starfish) also consume this but were not analyzed in depth... Actually, that could be a gap statement: \"Capsida Biotherapeutics and Lir's Starfish capsid-structure pipeline are additional consumers not assessed here\". Hmm, \"gaps\" likely means limitations of the research / missing evidence. I'll include both types briefly.\n\nNow, \"already_done\" for each:\n- Voyager: \"partly\" — they identified ALPL via Retrogenix cell microarray + ORFeome transduction screen, showed SPR KD ~20 nM, and demonstrated transcytosis in MDCK Transwell with human ALPL (>100-fold). They also solved cryo-EM structure of VCAP-102 (insert density at 3-fold apex of VR-IV) and noted \"100% conservation of predicted capsid-ALPL interacting residues\" (May 2026 presentation) — i.e., they have predicted capsid–ALPL contacts! But no exhaustive pose-depth enumeration across a capsid panel with transcytosis-vs-retention labels. So \"partly\".\n- Gradinaru/APPRAISE: \"partly\" — APPRAISE with 10 models/competition on 22 AAV variants vs LY6A + LY6C1/CA4 matrices; false negatives observed; Nat Commun 20245-model LRP6 complexes + cryo-ET; no depth-scaling / shared-motif panel test. Also the Jang 2022 structural basis paper modeled PHP.eB–LY6A complex (7WQO) — the card's check-first anchor. So \"partly\".\n- Dyno: \"unknown\" — proprietary; public claims of head-to-head ALPL capsid comparisons in mouse and NHP and structure-based design; no public record of exhaustive capsid–receptor pose enumeration. Status \"unknown\".\n\nLet me verify a few specific URLs for quotes I want to use:\n\nVoyager:\n- step_url: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 (Moyer 2025). step_quote: \"We identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model.\" — That's the abstract. Or a more \"step\" description: \"To understand the mechanism of action of VCAP-101/102 capsids and evaluate the translatability of our findings in humans, we sought to identify the vascular receptor used to transport capsids across the BBB.\" Better: combine — step = receptor identification + transcytosis validation for TRACER capsids. Quote: \"For this, we undertook two orthogonal approaches, a capsid binding screen using the Retrogenix cell microarray of human surface proteins and a transduction screen using cells stably transduced with a lentiviral human ORFeome library (Figures S6 A and S6B).\" Hmm. Or from the 10-K: \"We subsequently utilized these BBB-penetrant capsids to identify the receptors by which the capsids enter the BBB.\" — great \"own words\" step description. URL: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1640266/000110465926025197/vygr-20251231x10k.htm\n\nI'll use the 10-K quote for step and Moyer quotes for practice/already_done. Actually the step the card targets is more specifically: determining which capsid–receptor pairs transcytose vs remain intracellular — molecular mapping. Voyager's step: mechanism-of-action identification (receptor ID + transcytosis validation). Quote from 10-K is good for step: \"We subsequently utilized these BBB-penetrant capsids to identify the receptors by which the capsids enter the BBB. We have identified multiple receptors, including alkaline phosphatase, or ALPL, that are capable of transporting a large biologic molecule, such as a capsid, across the BBB.\"\n\n- activity: state active; claim: VY1706 (TRACER capsid gene therapy) advancing; IND Q2 2026; Q2 2026 results Aug 2026. Quote from Q2 2026 press release: \"Q4 2026: VY1706 expected to begin dosing in adults with early AD\". URL: https://ir.voyagertherapeutics.com/news-releases/news-release-details/voyager-reports-second-quarter-2026-financial-and-operating/ Also the 10-K: \"we anticipate the submission of an investigational new drug, or IND, application in the second quarter of 2026 and initiation of a clinical trial for the VY1706 program in the second half of 2026.\"\n\nHmm, need to make sure the quote from the Q2 2026 release is accurate — the search highlight showed bullets: \"- Q4 2026: VY1706 expected to begin dosing in adults with early AD\". I'll fetch that page to get exact sentences. Also the May 2026 presentation PDF quote: \"ALPL, a well conserved endothelial receptor, mediates VY1706 blood-brain barrier transport, supporting cross-species translatability\" and \"100% conservation of predicted capsid-ALPL interacting residues\" — great for \"predicted capsid-receptor interface\" practice. URL: https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/PRESENTATION_Voyager_IntravenousDeliveryofVY1706_May_2026_Final.pdf\n\n- practice claims: (a) scale of TRACER: \"the naive library contained approximately 4E7 individual capsid variants\" — from Moyer. (b) transcytosis assay: \"Ectopic expression of human ALPL induced a dramatic >100-fold increase in the transcytosis of VCAP-102 while it had no measurable impact on AAV9 migration (Figure 4 F).\" (c) SPR: \"showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (K D) of ∼20 nm at pH 7.4\". (d) They correlate capsid-receptor interaction with brain transduction: \"provide in vivo evidence that capsid-receptor interaction is directly correlated with brain transduction\".\n\n- if_lever_changes: if the exhaustive pose enumeration shows a shared 3-fold GPI-anchor engagement motif that separates transcytosing from retained pairs, Voyager could use the binding signature as an in silico filter on TRACER hits (e.g., the SPH motif family at VR-IV 456-458) before SPR/Transwell/in vivo, prioritizing ALPL-engaging geometries and deprioritizing retained ones; also inform NeuroShuttle receptor-binding molecule design.\n- if_not_changes: if pose depth doesn't matter (insert site fixes 3-fold geometry), Voyager keeps receptor ID via binding/transduction screens + transcytosis assays as-is; complex enumeration adds nothing to their Go/No-Go; no change to practice.\n- already_done: partly — Moyer 2025 identified ALPL and demonstrated transcytosis; and May 2026 deck mentions \"100% conservation of predicted capsid-ALPL interacting residues\" (so they predict capsid–receptor contacts) but no exhaustive pose-sampling study across capsid panel with retention labels.\n\nGradinaru/APPRAISE:\n- name: \"Gradinaru Lab (Caltech) — APPRAISE-AAV / receptor-directed AAV engineering\". URL: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE (or lab page). I'll use the GitHub repo as the programme URL since it's the open-source project, with lab attribution in name.\n- step: ranking capsid insertion peptides against receptors by AlphaFold-Multimer pairwise competition before in vivo work. step_url: https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adg6618 — wait, better to use the GitHub README or Ding 2024. step_quote from Shay 2023 methods: \"Each model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from each competition.\" Or from README: \"APPRAISE ... is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\" I'll use the README quote for \"in its own words\" + GitHub URL, and use Shay/Ding for practice quotes.\n\nActually \"Describe that step in the programme's own words, and copy the sentence from its own page that describes it (step_url, step_quote)\" — one step_quote. I'll use the APPRAISE README: \"Automated Pair-wise Peptide-Receptor binding model AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE) is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\" URL https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE.\n\n- activity: active — commits Apr 9, 2026 including \"distribution 1.2.6\"/\"1.3.0 package\" and \"Updating Colab notebook for compatibility to Boltz-1, Chai-1, and Openfold3\"; quote: \"Updating Colab notebook for compatibility to Boltz-1, Chai-1, and Openfold3\" commit on Apr 9, 2026; or README: \"Current APPRAISE supports backend-specific input generation for legacy ColabFold/AlphaFold-style FASTA files as well as newer open-source multimer tools including Boltz-1, Chai-1, and OpenFold3.\" URL: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE/commits/main/. Plus Cell Reports Oct 2025 paper. I'll use the commits page + quote \"Colab instruction update\" ... hmm better quote: commit title \"1.3.0 package\" (Apr 9, 2026) or the README sentence. For activity claim: \"APPRAISE repository shows commits on Apr 9, 2026 (1.3.0 package; Boltz-1/Chai-1/OpenFold3 compatibility), and the lab published AAV-hCA4-IV77 in Cell Reports on Oct 31, 2025.\" Quote from commits page: \"Updating Colab notebook for compatibility to Boltz-1, Chai-1, and Openfold3\" — that's a commit title, counts as a quote from the page. Or \"1.3.0 package\". I'll quote \"1.3.0 package\" with the commits URL. Hmm, a sentence would be better: from README \"Current APPRAISE supports backend-specific input generation for legacy ColabFold/AlphaFold-style FASTA files as well as newer open-source multimer tools including **Boltz-1**, **Chai-1**, and **OpenFold3**.\" — that describes recent release state. And claim cites Apr 9, 2026 commits. Both URLs same repo. OK.\n\n- practice: claims: (a) ten models per competition is the standard depth: \"Consequently, when AlphaFold-Multimer was used, 10 independent models were generated for each competition because AlphaFold-Multimer gives five different predictions for each input\" (Ding 2024, cell.com URL). (b) scale: \"The HT-APPRAISE screening took 24 h using three parallel Google Colaboratory GPU sessions and a laptop computer.\" (c) false negatives: \"Some false negatives were also observed, however, as in 9P08 with LY6A or 9P36 with CA-IV.\" (Shay 2023). (d) they've started using full AF-Multimer on LRP6 complexes with cryo-ET: \"We ran the Google Colaboratory notebook using an A100 SXM4 40GB GPU. Five structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles\" (Nat Commun 2024). (e) current pipeline uses receptor-directed selection + humanized mice: \"we implemented an in vitro pipeline with on-column and in-cell selections... to identify human CA-IV-engaging AAVs\" (Cell Reports 2025).\n\n- if_lever_changes: deeper pose sampling changing the competent-minus-retained gap would become a documented model-count default in APPRAISE (raise replicate count / add rigid-docking poses) and justify the shared 3-fold GPI-engagement motif as a classifier feature in their scoring (angle/depth already geometric; motif would add interface-anchor terms), letting them triage capsid–receptor pairs before humanized-mouse work and fix the observed false negatives.\n- if_not_changes: if gap is within 0.15 of the 144-model value, the lab keeps 10-model pairwise APPRAISE and its insert-site-fixed geometry assumption; no change to compute budget or scoring; \"insert location already fixes the 3-fold site\".\n- already_done: partly — they ran APPRAISE at 10 models on LY6A/LY6C1/CA4 matrices and validated PHP.eB–LY6A, observed false negatives; also 5-model LRP6 complex modeling + cryo-ET (Nat Commun 2024); no published pose-depth scaling study or transcytosis-vs-retention panel labels.\n\nDyno:\n- name: Dyno Therapeutics.\n- step: in silico/mechanism characterization of which BBB receptor each engineered capsid engages, feeding multi-mechanism CNS capsid portfolio. step_url: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-launches-dyno-yp2-a-top-performing-tfr1-mediated-aav-capsid-to-further-diversify-cns-delivery-portfolio. step_quote: \"Dyno's novel generative AI models, trained on billions of in vivo measurements, most recently accelerated with NVIDIA hardware and software, using Hopper GPUs on DGX Cloud and libraries such as TransformerEngine, integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms with exceptional efficiency.\" Or the practice step quote: \"Dyno is advancing capsids that engage additional BBB transport pathways, including several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno, as well as approaches involving alkaline phosphatase (ALPL), a compelling and distinct mechanism with translational relevance across species.\" The first is better for \"how it handles the step\" (tools). Actually the step = \"determine which capsid-receptor binding signatures yield BBB crossing\" → their own description of the step: \"Relying on any single biological pathway can potentially tie an effective therapeutic payload to a suboptimal delivery strategy... Dyno engineers capsids for high-potential BBB-crossing mechanisms and provides developers access to multiple capsids with distinct mechanisms\". Hmm. For step_quote I'll use the \"integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches...\" sentence since the card is about structural complex enumeration. Good.\n\n- activity: active — Dyno-yp2 launched Jan 12, 2026; Dyno-9zh launched 2026; Dyno-n96 \"Launched at ASGCT 2026\". Claim + quote from gene-delivery page: \"Launched in 2026, the Dyno-9zh is a top-performing capsid that achieves both exceptional brain transduction with significant liver detargeting in NHPs and mouse.\" URL https://www.dynotx.com/gene-delivery. Or the Jan 2026 press release date. I'll use the press release: quote \"January 12, 2026 ... today announced Dyno-yp2\" hmm. Use gene-delivery page quote for Dyno-9zh 2026 launch + press release for Dyno-yp2. One activity object only — I'll use the Jan 12, 2026 press release as most recent announcement... but Dyno-9zh/ASGCT 2026 is more recent (ASGCT 2026 ~May 2026). The gene-delivery page says \"Launched in 2026, the Dyno-9zh...\". I'll set activity claim: \"Active: Dyno launched the ALPL-targeting Dyno-9zh capsid in 2026 and the TfR1-targeting Dyno-yp2 on January 12, 2026, and lists ASGCT 2026 launches (Dyno-n96).\" Quote: \"Launched in 2026, the Dyno-9zh is a top-performing capsid that achieves both exceptional brain transduction with significant liver detargeting in NHPs and mouse.\" URL: https://www.dynotx.com/gene-delivery.\n\n- practice claims: (a) multi-mechanism portfolio: \"Dyno engineers capsids for high-potential BBB-crossing mechanisms and provides developers access to multiple capsids with distinct mechanisms, creating strategic delivery agility\" (Jan 2026 release). (b) ALPL head-to-head validation: \"Best capsid when compared head-to-head with other ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP.\" (gene-delivery page). (c) scale: \"Dyno's Low-Shot Efficient Accelerated Performance (LEAP SM) technology, paired with the capability to make billions of in vivo sequence-function measurements each month\" (Roche 2024 release). (d) partners: Roche $50M upfront Oct 2024 (neurological). \n\n- if_lever_changes: a validated shared 3-fold GPI-engagement signature would become a design filter in Dyno's sequence+structure generative pipeline for new BBB mechanisms (they already target ALPL — GPI-anchored — and discovered receptors), letting them rank generated capsids by predicted transcytosis-competent geometry and cut in vivo screening; would also strengthen the ALPL mechanistic story for licensing.\n- if_not_changes: if pose depth doesn't change the call, Dyno continues to rely on in vivo phenotype (LEAP billions of measurements) and receptor engagement assays; structural complex enumeration would not enter their Go/No-Go; no change.\n- already_done: unknown — proprietary; public evidence shows receptor mechanism validation in vivo and head-to-head ALPL capsid comparisons but no public exhaustive capsid–receptor pose enumeration; claim carefully worded.\n\nNow gaps:\n1. No published pose-depth (model-count) scaling study for AAV capsid–receptor complexes; closest is 10 models/competition (Ding 2024) and 5 models with 20 recycles (Shay 2024).\n2. Phenotype labels for \"transcytosed vs retained\" per capsid–receptor pair are sparse: Voyager's MDCK Transwe