recision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\"\n\nPractice quotes for Gradinaru:\n1. Ding 2024 (cell.com S1525-0016(24)00219-3): \"Consequently, when AlphaFold-Multimer was used, 10 independent models were generated for each competition because AlphaFold-Multimer gives five different predictions for each input, while ESMFold produced two different models for each competition.\"\n2. Shay 2023 Sci Adv (PMC10115422 or science.org): \"Each model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from each competition.\" and \"Some false negatives were also observed, however, as in 9P08 with LY6A or 9P36 with CA-IV.\"\n3. Ding 2024: \"The HT-APPRAISE screening took 24 h using three parallel Google Colaboratory GPU sessions and a laptop computer.\"\n4. Nat Commun 2024 (nature.com s41467-024-52149-0): \"Five structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles, and MSA generated with MMseqs2 (UniRef+Environmental) and templates from PDB70.\" and the PBMEC: \"we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Fig. 6a, b).\"\n\nalready_done for Gradinaru: partly. Claim: they ran the closest prior experiment — APPRAISE matrices at 10 AF-Multimer models per competition on LY6A/LY6C1/CA4, validated PHP.eB–LY6A poses and reported false negatives — but no pose-depth scaling (144–2,880) and no shared 3-fold GPI-motif test against transcytosis-vs-retention labels. Quote: \"Some false negatives were also observed, however, as in 9P08 with LY6A or 9P36 with CA-IV.\" URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10115422/ or science.org. I'll use PMC10115422 as it's fetchable... Actually the search result listed the PMC URL. Fine.\n\nAlso for the PHP.eB–LY6A pose validation (the card's check-first anchor, PDB 7WQO): Jang et al. 2022 \"Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB\" (Mol Ther Methods Clin Dev). I didn't verify 7WQO or the RMSD numbers; those are in the card itself. I don't need to re-cite them, but for \"already_done\" I could mention that the lab published the PHP.eB–LY6A structural model. Let me not overclaim; cite Shay/Ding.\n\nDyno practice quotes:\n1. Jan 2026 Dyno-yp2 release: \"Dyno is advancing capsids that engage additional BBB transport pathways, including several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno, as well as approaches involving alkaline phosphatase (ALPL), a compelling and distinct mechanism with translational relevance across species.\" URL: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-launches-dyno-yp2-a-top-performing-tfr1-mediated-aav-capsid-to-further-diversify-cns-delivery-portfolio\n2. Gene delivery page: \"Best capsid when compared head-to-head with other ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP.\" URL: https://www.dynotx.com/gene-delivery\n3. Roche 2024 release: \"Dyno's Low-Shot Efficient Accelerated Performance (LEAP SM) technology, paired with the capability to make billions of in vivo sequence-function measurements each month, enables optimization of capsids for therapeutic success at an accelerated rate.\" URL: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-forms-new-strategic-partnership-with-roche-to-advance-aav-gene-therapy-vectors-for-neurological-diseases\n4. Step quote: \"Dyno's novel generative AI models, trained on billions of in vivo measurements, most recently accelerated with NVIDIA hardware and software, using Hopper GPUs on DGX Cloud and libraries such as TransformerEngine, integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms with exceptional efficiency.\"\n\nDyno already_done: unknown — claim: no public record of exhaustive capsid–receptor pose enumeration; public work validates mechanisms phenotypically (head-to-head ALPL capsid comparisons in mouse and NHP) and states structure-based design is integrated with generative AI, but pose-depth studies are unpublished. Quote: \"Best capsid when compared head-to-head with other ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP.\" URL gene-delivery.\n\nVoyager practice quotes:\n1. Moyer 2025: \"For this, we undertook two orthogonal approaches, a capsid binding screen using the Retrogenix cell microarray of human surface proteins and a transduction screen using cells stably transduced with a lentiviral human ORFeome library\" — step of receptor ID.\n2. Moyer 2025: \"We established a capsid transcytosis model using Madin-Darby canine kidney cells (MDCK) in a Transwell migration assay. Ectopic expression of human ALPL induced a dramatic >100-fold increase in the transcytosis of VCAP-102 while it had no measurable impact on AAV9 migration (Figure 4 F).\"\n3. Moyer 2025 scale: \"After the initial library cloning and virus production, the naive library contained approximately 4E7 individual capsid variants as identified by next generation sequencing (NGS).\"\n4. May 2026 deck: \"100% conservation of predicted capsid-ALPL interacting residues\" (they already model predicted capsid–receptor contacts).\n5. SPR: \"showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (K D) of ∼20 nm at pH 7.4\".\n\nVoyager step (own words): receptor identification + transcytosis validation for TRACER capsids. step_url: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9. step_quote: \"We identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model.\"\n\nOr use the 10-K sentence: \"We subsequently utilized these BBB-penetrant capsids to identify the receptors by which the capsids enter the BBB.\" — this is more \"pipeline step\". I'll use the Moyer abstract quote as step_quote since it's the step description in their paper; and describe in \"step\" field.\n\nActivity Voyager: state active. claim: \"Active: VY1706, a TRACER-derived capsid gene therapy, is expected to begin dosing in adults with early AD in Q4 2026, with AAIC 2026 NHP GLP tox data presented, and ALPL-VYGR-NeuroShuttle sits in the wholly-owned research pipeline.\" url: https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/pipeline-overview/ quote: \"At AAIC 2026, Voyager presented 6-month good laboratory practice (GLP) toxicology non-human primate (NHP) data showing VY1706 was well tolerated and resulted in sustained tau protein reduction by up to 75% in key AD brain regions following a single intravenous (IV) dose.\" Plus \"Status: Expected to begin dosing in adults with early AD in Q4 2026.\" — one quote field; I'll use the AAIC 2026 sentence and include status in claim... better to use the Status sentence as quote: \"Status: Expected to begin dosing in adults with early AD in Q4 2026.\" Hmm. I'll use: \"At AAIC 2026, Voyager presented 6-month good laboratory practice (GLP) toxicology non-human primate (NHP) data showing VY1706 was well tolerated and resulted in sustained tau protein reduction by up to 75% in key AD brain regions following a single intravenous (IV) dose.\" with claim mentioning Q4 2026 dosing. Or two claims in practice. The activity object has one quote. Fine.\n\nif_lever_changes (Voyager): If exhaustive pose enumeration shows a shared 3-fold GPI-receptor anchor geometry separating transcytosing from retained pairs, Voyager could add that binding signature as an in silico gate on TRACER hits (and on NeuroShuttle binder design), ranking variants by predicted transcytosis-competent engagement of ALPL before SPR/Transwell/NHP work; the ALPL mechanism story for VY1706/Gen2 capsids would gain a structural signature that supports cross-species translation.\nif_not_changes (Voyager): If deeper sampling changes nothing (insert position at VR-IV/VR-VIII already fixes 3-fold engagement), Voyager keeps receptor ID by cell-microarray/ORFeome screens and Transwell transcytosis validation; pose enumeration would not enter Go/No-Go; no